jaffle shop corpus — what the data is, and the license that ships with it
=========================================================================

What the data is
----------------
The Jaffle Shop corpus this directory ships is derived from a run made by the maintainers
of this repository, once, on 2026-09-24, by
running jafgen 0.4.14 (the jaffle shop generator, github.com/dbt-labs/jaffle-shop-generator,
tag v0.4.14) as `jafgen 1 --pre raw`. The run's first calendar quarter was then cut out by
derive/trim.py, and the later steps of the derivation chain in PROVENANCE move it in time,
make it European and add addresses. PROVENANCE records the exact run, the trim rule and
the chain. Apart from those later steps, every row is a row of that run's output, byte for
byte; the trim only leaves rows out.

They are our own output: they were not copied from any dataset published by dbt Labs or
anyone else, and they are not regenerated by anything in this repository.

One file is not jafgen output at all: customer_addresses.csv. It holds one synthetic
Utrecht address per customer, derived by derive/addresses.py from SHA-256 hashes of the
customer ids and lists embedded in that script (invented street names and Dutch postal
code parts). These addresses are our own synthetic additions; they describe no real person
or residence.

derive/localize.py replaced jafgen's text to set the corpus in Europe, and where it named a real brand
or person: the customer names, the store names and tax rates, and some product and supply
names and descriptions. It computed the VAT of every order again. Those names and rates
are our own additions.

The rows are synthetic. Identifiers, timestamps, order contents and subtotals come from
the generator's random simulation. What the simulation draws from is fixed text in
jafgen's own source code: the product and supply names, descriptions, prices and costs
that localize.py left alone, and the sentences the tweets are assembled from. None of it
describes a real person, order or store.

